id: pm-flnefm
title: "The graph audit raises duplicate dependency rows at warning severity and the only remediation available deletes every copy, so collapsing a duplicate means briefly destroying a real edge"
description: "The graph audit reports duplicate_dependency_row at warning severity, the highest severity it emits, and the mutation surface has no operation that resolves it. Reproduced end to end on this workspace during the 2026-07-28 review pass.\n\nSetup. pm-dkrmzv carried two identical rows toward the same target: pm-nfrhf0 implements at 04:20:29 and pm-nfrhf0 implements at 10:02:34. Same source, same target, same kind. The audit correctly flagged it as duplicate_dependency_row with severity warning.\n\nAttempted remediation. pm update pm-dkrmzv --dep-remove id=pm-nfrhf0,kind=implements. The documented selector grammar for dep-remove is id or id=<id>,kind=<value>,source_kind=<value>, which is the most precise selector the flag accepts. It removed both rows. The item was left with no implements edge toward pm-nfrhf0 at all, and the audit went clean by deleting a real relationship rather than by collapsing a duplicate.\n\nRecovery required a second mutation to re-add the edge, so the workspace passed through a state where a relationship the graph depends on did not exist, and the history now records a removal and an addition rather than a deduplication. On a workspace where an agent is repairing many duplicates in one pass, or where another agent reads the graph between the two writes, that window is not academic.\n\nThe gap is precise. Selector semantics are defensible on their own terms: a selector selects every match, and remove-all is what a selector should do. The defect is that this is the only remediation offered for a condition the audit raises at warning severity, so the tool asks for a repair it provides no safe operation to perform. Every other governance finding this project raises has a remediation path that does not pass through deleting correct data.\n\nThree shapes would close it, in increasing generality. A dedupe intent on the mutation surface that collapses identical rows to one and is a no-op when there is nothing to collapse. An occurrence-scoped selector so a specific row can be named. Or write-time idempotence for exact-duplicate dependency rows, which would prevent the condition rather than remediate it, and would also mean the audit finding could only ever describe legacy data.\n\nThe last option deserves weight. This duplicate was created by an ordinary append: the edge already existed from an earlier pass, a later pass appended the same edge again, and nothing objected. update --dep appending is correct and deliberate behaviour for distinct rows. Appending a byte-identical row is the case where append and idempotence disagree, and the audit already declares which side it is on by calling the result a warning.\n\nBoundaries. pm-rggtvd owns hierarchy-canonicalizing kinds bypassing cycle detection, which is a different write-time canonicalization problem. pm-4020c5 owns the fragmented kind lexicon. pm-6x7o owns the governance audit's own scope. None of them owns the absence of a safe remediation for a finding the audit already raises."
type: Issue
